00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 You may have noticed that these videos have a similar structure to last week.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
 We're now on the third of three basic components in rotating systems.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 And just like last week we met masses, we met springs, and now we're going to meet frictions.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 This is intentional. I'm trying to draw as many kind of threads back to what we've already done as possible.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 So you can start to see under the hood, start to see the analogs.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 Friction, I think in rotating systems is pretty easy to visualize.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 You can imagine taking a shaft even on high quality bearings and spinning it up.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 And eventually that thing is going to come to a stop, right?

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 As all of that kinetic energy is dissipated into heat from the rollers in the bearings.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 In this course, we're going to use friction elements, which we give this symbol to model these kinds of losses.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 Now a prominent kind of real world example of this would be something like a gearbox.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 In this course, we're going to model gearboxes as ideal.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 We're going to assume that all the power we put in we then get out again.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 But if we ever want to model the non-ideality, the reality that in gearboxes, when you put a certain amount of power in you only get a fraction of that power out due to friction,

15
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 we'll dump a friction element on one side of the gearbox to model that kind of loss.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 Another real world example would be something like your car.

17
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 In Australia, we're pretty fond of quoting how many kilowatts our car makes.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 But if you look on the car sales website, I'm pretty sure what they're quoting is the amount of power that your car makes at the crank,

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 at the kind of output of the engine.

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
 But between the engine and the wheels, it has to go through a transmission at gearbox.

21
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 If it's a rear-wheel-drive car, it goes through a prop shaft and then through a differential and then through some drive shafts and all throughout that process,

22
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 there's friction involved that's sapping that power away to the tune of, I don't know, 10, 15% of the power that you're making at the crank.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 In this course, we're going to use a linear viscous friction model, similar to what we did for translating mechanical systems.

24
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 And actually, it has a pretty similar CCR. Here it is.

25
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 TB, in this case, is the torque that's produced by the friction element,

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 usually in the direction opposing motion.

27
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 B is the linear viscous damping coefficient.

28
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 And on my URL is the kind of relative velocity, the relative angular velocity between our shaft and whatever kind of the reference surface is.

29
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
 Now, in the same way as the car example last week,

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 where we had a car sliding on a plane and we defined the V-rail for the friction to be the velocity of that car minus the velocity of the ground, which was zero.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 For rotating systems, we'll define omega-rail as the velocity of the shaft that the friction element lives on minus zero.

32
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 Because in this case, the reference surface is probably the outer race of our bearing.

33
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 The thing which is fixed to, I don't know, sometimes bearing housing, which isn't moving.

34
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 If we do it this way, then dealing with the SSR for friction, the definition of omega-rail and the associated drawing of a friction torque on a free body diagram is actually really easy.

35
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 Same as before, you only need to remember that friction always opposes motion.

36
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 And then you should draw it opposite to the coordinate system of any inertia that you happen during a free body diagram for.

37
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 So, with all of that said, I think we now have enough information,

38
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 we've now met enough components that we can go and model problem 2.4, our first rotating mechanical system.
